---
import SabGuide from '../../components/sab/SabGuide.astro';
import { getGuide } from '../../lib/guides';

const guide = getGuide('volunteering-alberta-high-school');
---

<SabGuide guide={guide}>
  <p>
    Most advice about volunteering in high school is about hours. Committees do not count hours; they read for whether you stayed. One placement held for two years says more than eight things tried once, and it is also less work. This is where Alberta students can actually get placed, and which of it is open right now rather than in theory.
  </p>

  <h2>Hospitals and health care</h2>
  <p>
    This is the most-asked and least-answered one. <a href="/programs/ahs-youth-volunteer-research-programs/">Alberta Health Services runs youth volunteer and research placements</a> in regional hospitals and clinics, with direct exposure to clinical environments and health administration. Intake is ongoing rather than a single annual deadline, so the question is whether your site has openings, not whether the window is closed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two things worth knowing before you apply. Placements usually carry a minimum commitment measured in months, which is the point rather than the obstacle. And they are the single most useful thing on an application for a health-related award or program, because they are the rare line a committee can verify. If you are aiming at medicine, read the <a href="/guides/medical-experience-high-school-alberta/">medical experience guide</a> alongside this.
  </p>

  <h2>Science centres and public science</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/programs/telus-spark-science-centre-volunteering-calgary/">TELUS Spark in Calgary</a> takes volunteers to work alongside its science communicators, running workshops, staffing exhibits and supporting programs. It is ongoing intake, and it is the closest thing in the province to a standing science-communication apprenticeship for a teenager.
  </p>
  <p>
    Shifts are the unglamorous part and the reason it works: a student who has explained the same exhibit forty times can explain anything, and that is a skill an interview panel notices long before it notices a list of clubs.
  </p>

  <h2>Civic and policy work</h2>
  <p>
    If your interest is closer to government than to a lab, the <a href="/programs/city-of-edmonton-youth-council/">City of Edmonton Youth Council</a> is an official civic agency rather than a simulation: members research and write policy proposals that go to City Council. That distinction matters on an application, because a real proposal to a real council is evidence and a model parliament is practice.
  </p>
  <p>
    Further out, the <a href="/programs/ocean-networks-canada-youth-advisory-council/">Ocean Networks Canada Youth Advisory Council</a> is open to Albertans and pairs environmental work with real data analysis, which makes it unusually good preparation for a research application later.
  </p>

  <h2>Teaching what you already know</h2>
  <p>
    The most underrated category, because it converts a subject you are already good at into service:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/programs/future-creators-university-of-alberta/">Future Creators at the University of Alberta</a> is a student-run group taking free STEM kits and mentoring out to grades 5 to 12.</li>
    <li><a href="/programs/hackergal-national-ambassador-program/">Hackergal's National Ambassador Program</a> is built for Grade 9 to 12 girls and gender-diverse students who have aged out of the beginner programs and want to run them instead.</li>
    <li><a href="/programs/ja-southern-alberta-company-program/">JA Southern Alberta's Company Program</a> is 20 weeks of building and running a real company with a mentor, which is service and enterprise in the same line.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>If you want the hours counted formally</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/programs/the-duke-of-edinburghs-international-award/">The Duke of Edinburgh's International Award</a> is non-competitive and structural: you log hours across physical recreation, a skill, voluntary service and an adventurous journey, and the award is earned rather than won. Nobody is turned down for being a weaker candidate. If you are volunteering anyway, registering means the same hours also produce a recognised international credential.
  </p>

  <h2>The part that pays</h2>
  <p>
    Community involvement is not just a character note; it is the explicit criterion on a large share of the directory. Some examples across quite different scales:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/scholarships/td-scholarships-for-community-leadership/">TD Scholarships for Community Leadership</a>, worth up to $70,000, which is national and genuinely competitive.</li>
    <li><a href="/scholarships/clay-riddell-community-hero-scholarship/">The Clay Riddell Community Hero Scholarship</a> in Calgary, $8,500 to $10,000.</li>
    <li><a href="/scholarships/calgary-flames-foundation-community-involvement-scholarship/">The Calgary Flames Foundation Community Involvement Scholarship</a>, $2,000, for Grade 12 CBE and Calgary Catholic students with a volunteer record.</li>
    <li><a href="/scholarships/kelly-creaghan-stepping-up-in-the-community-award/">The Kelly Creaghan Stepping Up in the Community Award</a>, $2,000, province-wide.</li>
    <li><a href="/scholarships/south-country-co-op-scholarship/">The South Country Co-op Scholarship</a>, $1,000, in Medicine Hat.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    That last one is the pattern worth noticing. The national award has the biggest number and the worst odds; the local one has a small number and a pool you can count. <a href="/guides/local-scholarships-better-odds/">Local awards</a> is the argument for weighting your effort toward the bottom of that list, not the top.
  </p>

  <h2>What to do about it</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Pick one placement with ongoing intake</strong> and start now rather than in Grade 12. AHS, TELUS Spark and JA all take students continuously; there is no window to wait for.</li>
    <li><strong>Log it as you go.</strong> Dates, hours, who supervised you, one sentence on what you actually did. Reconstructing two years of this the night before a deadline is how a real record turns into vague claims.</li>
    <li><strong>Register for Duke of Edinburgh</strong> if you are already putting in hours, because the same effort then also produces a credential.</li>
    <li><strong>Ask your supervisor for a reference in the spring</strong>, not in October with everyone else. The <a href="/guides/reference-letters-for-scholarships/">reference letter guide</a> covers what to hand them.</li>
  </ol>

  <p class="sabg-note">
    Programs move: intakes pause, councils change their terms, and a placement that was open last year may not be. Every listing linked here links to the provider's own page. Confirm there before you plan around it.
  </p>
</SabGuide>
